<Docs
  title="Two-way sync"
  description="Keep working in the task list you already use. Sync is opt-in and bolt-on: the local database stays authoritative, and the app is fully usable without it."
  toc={toc}
>
  <h2 id="offline">Offline first</h2>
  <p>
    Your tasks live in a local database on your machine, and that database is what the app reads and
    writes. Sync is a layer on top of it: when you connect an outside system, the app maps your tasks onto
    it and reconciles changes in both directions.
  </p>
  <p>
    The practical consequence is that a sync that fails does not break the app. Nothing in the task loop
    requires a network call, so you keep working offline and the mapping catches up when it can.
  </p>

  <h2 id="todoist">Todoist</h2>
  <p>
    Todoist is the primary external task list, and it is the integration to reach for if you want two-way
    sync today. <Badge tone="success" variant="subtle">available</Badge>
  </p>
  <p>
    Connect your account from the app's integrations pane. Once connected, changes you make in Todoist
    arrive locally and changes you make locally are pushed back, with the local database remaining the copy
    the app trusts.
  </p>
  <p>
    Each Todoist project maps onto a local project tag, and its sections become child tags beneath it, so
    the structure you built in Todoist survives the move. Todoist priorities map onto urgency — P1 lands
    as the most urgent, P4 as the least — and due dates become deadlines. If both sides changed the same
    task, the newer edit wins; a task deleted on one side disappears on the other rather than coming back.
  </p>

  <h2 id="github">GitHub</h2>
  <p>
    GitHub issues and projects are in preview. <Badge tone="accent" variant="subtle">preview</Badge> The
    same sync surface is there — authenticate in the integrations pane and your issues and projects map
    onto your lists — but expect the shape of that mapping to change while it settles.
  </p>
  <p>
    GitHub also has a second, separate role: a coding workflow run can use a real checkout, which is what
    makes the diffs in <a href="/docs/workflows">Semi-automated workflows</a> real.
  </p>

  <h2 id="what-syncs">What syncs</h2>
  <p>
    Titles, descriptions, and completion state are what flow today; the exact contract is written down
    rather than implied. If you need to know whether a specific field survives a round trip, the
    integration's spec is the answer — see <a href="/docs/contributor/integrations">Integrations</a>.
  </p>

  <Callout tone="note" title="Setting this up">
    Accounts and credentials are configured in the app, not in a file. See
    <a href="/docs/configuration">Configuration</a> for the integrations pane, and
    <a href="/docs/status">Feature status</a> for what is shipped versus planned.
  </Callout>
</Docs>
